00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.800
each person finds their own 10
secrets to language learning, things

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.910
that they, uh, find help them become
more effective language learners.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:08.280
But the absolute first one is the
famous three keys that I often quote

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.579
the, uh, again, I don't remember her
name, but she was the director of

00:02:11.579 --> 00:02:15.630
the languages department at the San
Diego, San Diego state university.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:19.305
And, and she said, you know, language
learning consists of three things, your

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:23.385
attitude or the attitude of the learner,
the time spent with the language.

00:02:23.415 --> 00:02:27.055
In other words, not sitting in a
classroom, listening to explanations

00:02:27.055 --> 00:02:29.945
in your own language about the
language, but actually being with the

00:02:29.945 --> 00:02:31.445
language, listening, reading, speaking.

00:02:31.955 --> 00:02:35.975
So time with the language, attitude, time
with language, and then this ability to

00:02:35.975 --> 00:02:37.895
notice what's happening in the language.

00:02:37.925 --> 00:02:42.095
Some people will hear the language and
just not notice how it's pronounced.

00:02:42.215 --> 00:02:45.515
They'll continue pronouncing it the
way they think it should be pronounced

00:02:45.545 --> 00:02:50.460
maybe based on the writing system
in their own language or whatever.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.620
So we do have to be a little
attentive to the language.

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:57.000
So that's secret number one is
really, you know, buy into these

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.980
three keys to language learning.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:02.820
The second thing is, you know, you got to
take charge of your own language learning.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.720
You can't be dependent on a teacher
or expecting a school to teach you.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.670
You have to drive the program.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:09.930
What are you interested in?

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:11.400
Why are you doing it?

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:16.575
And then, you know, find things
that you find, uh, worthwhile

00:03:16.725 --> 00:03:18.195
doing and then do them.

00:03:18.945 --> 00:03:22.345
The third thing is words are
the key to language learning.

00:03:22.345 --> 00:03:24.795
And that's  why at LingQ we track
how many words, you know, how

00:03:24.795 --> 00:03:25.875
many words you have looked up,

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:26.925
how many words you've read.

00:03:27.615 --> 00:03:31.605
Because again, when I was correcting
people's English, it wasn't

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.890
find points of grammar that made
their English less effective.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.780
It was how they used words.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:38.010
They didn't have enough words.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.880
They didn't know which words belonged
with which other words, the whole thing

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.850
about languages is acquiring words.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:49.710
And then getting used to how they
work, getting a sense of, you

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.170
know, the patterns of the language.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.050
And that's all based on words,
words, and phrases, but phrases are

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.510
patterns, which consists of words.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:01.020
Uh, also when you go to speak.

00:04:01.665 --> 00:04:03.765
Again, you're speaking
words, you're speaking.

00:04:03.765 --> 00:04:08.325
Meaning don't worry about how close
to native pronunciation you may have.

00:04:08.565 --> 00:04:10.785
Of course, you're paying
attention to how the native speak.

00:04:10.935 --> 00:04:12.045
You're trying to imitate it.

00:04:12.315 --> 00:04:15.105
Some people get closer, some
people aren't quite as close.

00:04:15.135 --> 00:04:18.885
It doesn't matter if you're
using words skillfully.

00:04:18.885 --> 00:04:23.205
If you're using words, if you're
developing that feel for how words are

00:04:23.205 --> 00:04:28.275
used in the language time, I mentioned
time, make the time, find the time.

00:04:28.815 --> 00:04:32.835
And in that sense, listening is such a
powerful activity because it's helping

00:04:32.835 --> 00:04:34.215
your brain get used to the language.

00:04:34.545 --> 00:04:40.395
And it's also something that you can
fit in almost at all times of the day,

00:04:40.395 --> 00:04:44.595
while in the car, on public transit or
washing the dishes and so forth and so on.

00:04:45.765 --> 00:04:47.505
Find ways to motivate yourself.

00:04:47.715 --> 00:04:48.075
Okay.

00:04:48.075 --> 00:04:50.505
It might be the kind of
content you're doing.

00:04:50.535 --> 00:04:53.235
Maybe you're motivated by doing
spaced repetition systems,

00:04:53.595 --> 00:04:55.695
find things that motivate you.

00:04:57.075 --> 00:05:00.435
Another thing is don't worry too much
about how long it's going to take.

00:05:00.465 --> 00:05:05.865
If your focus is on enjoying the
process, then that how long it

00:05:05.865 --> 00:05:07.755
takes will take care of itself.

00:05:07.935 --> 00:05:12.435
You will move along more quickly if you're
focused on enjoying the process, rather

00:05:12.435 --> 00:05:16.935
than if you're focused on how quickly,
you know, can I learn the language.

00:05:17.685 --> 00:05:21.885
Another secret I think is to
not be too focused on how much

00:05:21.885 --> 00:05:22.844
you can say at the beginning.

00:05:23.085 --> 00:05:24.284
It's fun to speak.

00:05:24.495 --> 00:05:25.755
It's fun to get feedback.

00:05:25.755 --> 00:05:28.034
It's fun to interact with
people in a new language.

00:05:28.034 --> 00:05:31.875
It's fun to try to put some words together
in a language that we're learning, but

00:05:31.875 --> 00:05:36.375
ultimately what matters is how well can
you speak in six months or 12 months.

00:05:36.615 --> 00:05:41.594
And that sense of major focus on
input, acquiring words, is going

00:05:41.594 --> 00:05:45.840
to be more effective than using
the limited vocabulary that you've

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:47.610
acquired over and over and over again.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:51.570
You have to expand beyond that limit
range, limited range of vocabulary

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:57.210
in order to engage with, uh, native
speakers in meaningful conversation.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:02.580
Uh, and, and finally, you know, learning
a language is a, it's a process of

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.220
immersing yourself in a new environment.

00:06:05.805 --> 00:06:09.794
The really, as I've said before, I'm not
sure there is a chapter one, chapter 10.

00:06:09.825 --> 00:06:10.305
Yes.

00:06:10.695 --> 00:06:13.275
Obviously, if you have more
repetition, it's going to be easier.

00:06:13.425 --> 00:06:16.544
But at some point you're at,
you're looking at this lake or this

00:06:16.544 --> 00:06:20.294
swimming pool, you just have to get
in and get wet, start somewhere,

00:06:20.385 --> 00:06:23.685
start listening and reading, start
using the little bit that you can.

00:06:24.135 --> 00:06:25.065
Immerse yourself.

00:06:25.695 --> 00:06:29.265
And if you do those things, I think you'll
be successful as a language learner.

